task: growing-default
agent: opus5-bench
started: 2026-09-08
branch: feature/growing-default
worktree: ../okay-wt-default

why: operator decision 2026-09-08 — make `growing` the default buffer
  behind `Channel.apply`, and give exact FIFO its own builder for
  callers who need it.

  The case is now measured. At an equal memory budget `growing` ties
  the ring at one producer (171 against 169) and is 2.4x and 7.2x
  ahead at four and sixteen. `adaptive` as the default is REFUTED by
  the same run: it splits the budget into parts, so a lone producer
  gets 64 slots of 1024 and reads 1119 — 6.6x the ring.

  That is exactly the hazard `Channel.apply`'s own comment names:
  "a lone producer must not pay for parts it never opens". `growing`
  is the answer to it — until a second producer appears it IS the
  ring.

what, in the order the code itself demands:
  1. The A/B that comment requires and that has never been run: every
     single-producer path the default FEEDS — `Channel.buffer`,
     `bufferChunked`, `merge` — with the ring against growing,
     alternating, on a quiet box. Existing lanes exercise all three
     (MergeBenchmark, ChunkFlushBenchmark, ScalingBenchmark), and they
     go through `Channel.apply`, so a variant build measures them.
  2. `Queues.strong[A].fifo(capacity)` — the ring under a name that
     says what it gives. It exists today as `bounded`, which does not.
  3. Switch the default, and say in the CONTRACT that exact order
     between producers is no longer promised by the default and is
     `fifo`'s job.
  4. Re-measure and update every table the change touches.

predict: the three paths are single-producer, so growing never grows
  in them and should read within noise of the ring.
  DISQUALIFYING: if any of the three regresses beyond noise, the
  switch does NOT happen on my say-so — I report the number and the
  operator decides again with it. A default that is 7x better at
  sixteen producers is not worth a measurable tax on the single
  producer every caller starts with.
